&nbsp;


Dear all,


&nbsp;


I hope you are well and are enjoying the half term break.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


I write in relation three matters, one of which we require your instructions in relation to:


&nbsp;




an update on the second week of trial;




an application by the Ford Defendants to amend some of their pleadings -
we require your instructions in relation to this;&nbsp;and




the discontinuance of Mr Gormley (as previously discussed in my update of 26 September 2025.


&nbsp;


Second and third week of trial


&nbsp;


Week two of the PDD Trial focused on the issue of what are conditions which are “reasonably likely to be encountered in normal vehicle operation and use”
 for the purposes of identifying prohibited defeat devices in the Defendants’ vehicles. The Court heard from experts in the field of what constitutes &quot;normal driving&quot; and climatology. For the Claimants, our experts were Professor Tate (normal driving) and Professor
 Mills (climatology) and for the Defendants, Professor Hausberger (normal driving) and Professor Chapman (climatology).&nbsp;


&nbsp;


Ultimately, while this was important evidence which will underpin the definition of &quot;prohibited defeat device&quot;, as the Judge remarked at the end of the evidence
 - it would not be a central part of her ultimate judgment.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


That said, and as we have advised our insurers; we think that the Claimants’ evidence and cross-examination of the Defendants’ experts has clearly had a positive
 impact on the judge’s views of this element of the case. While we anticipate that the likely approach Lady Justice Cockerill will take is to set a limit on the range of conditions which would reasonably be encountered during normal vehicle operation and use,
 however, we do not expect that she will be inclined to adopt an excessively restrictive set of boundaries (which the Defendants have advocated for) in light of the Claimants’ case on this issue.


&nbsp;


Starting tomorrow, Mercedes kicks us off into the manufacturer specific sections of the trial. Mercedes will run from tomorrow, 29 October 2025, until 6 November.
 Ford then starts on 10 November and runs until 18 November. Preparations for Ford are going well. As previously offered, if any of you wish to attend on the 10,11,12,13, 17 or 18 November, please let me know. You are most welcome to attend and we can arrange
 for this to happen.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


Defendants' application to amend


&nbsp;


On Friday afternoon, we received correspondence from the Ford Defendants, proposing amendments to two of their Individual Defences. Their reason for this
 exceptionally late application, they have said, is that they have given &quot;careful consideration to the fidelity of their pleadings in light of those [opening] submissions, and have taken the view that two of the IDEFS....out properly to be amended.&quot;
 The effect of these amendments is that they are seeking to change their case on a central point of dispute between the parties. As summarised by one of the Claimants legal team - these proposed amendments mean &quot;at the very lease, their legal representatives
 (and their mechanical expert) were misled (though they would very likely say they were misinformed)&quot;.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


While we would be within our right to refuse these amendments, having taken advice from our counsel team, we are minded to permit the Defendants to have these
 amendments, on the proviso that they pay our costs on the indemnity basis. Costs on the indemnity basis in essence mean that becuase the conduct of the paying party has been
unreasonable, the burden to prove that the costs claimed is less. In practical terms, it means that the costs recovered are generally higher than otherwise. Counsel have also advised that it is in our favour to permit this as it allows us to put under
 the Judge's nose that the Ds have in essence lied, or at least not been fully truthful, about the software in their vehicles.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


We have written to the Defendants to propose this, but ultimately we must also take your instructions on this. It is our advice that, in the event Ford agree
 to pay our wasted costs, we will consent to the application. Can you please confirm by return that you instruct us to consent to the application?


&nbsp;


Mr Gormley


&nbsp;


As you may recall from my 26 September update and from the instructions you provided, you instructed us to take the following steps:


&nbsp;


1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
Proceed to discontinue Mr Gormley’s claim in England &amp; Wales; and

2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
Remove Mr Gormley from the Ford Group Register.

We are in the process of doing so. We have liaised with the ATE&nbsp; provider on this as there are, as advised, necessarily costs associated with this. The insurers
 have confirmed that they will cost the costs of this discontinuance, but reserve the right to seek them from Pogust Goodhead or the client at the conclusion of the matter is so required.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


We will now proceed with the discontinuance of Mr Gormely's claim. No further action is required form you in respect of this.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


Summary


&nbsp;


Please confirm your instructions to me on the Defendants' application to amend.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


I will endeavour to provide you with an update in advance of Ford being heard, however it may not be until the end of the Ford aspect of the trial that I
 am able to update you.


&nbsp;


If you have any questions about any of this update, please let me know.


&nbsp;


Kind regards,


&nbsp;


Michael &amp; the Ford Litigation Team


