s unchanged.&nbsp;&nbsp;RCKC’s position appears to be that while he accepts some of the clauses may be problematic (notably 5.49 c) which contains representations that have been made, and possibly are continuing to be made, on behalf of the firm, as being in our client’s best interests, he considered concerns to be (a) hypothetical with a low likelihood of arising; and (b) curable by a protocol. However: &nbsp;&nbsp;(a) This advice, as we understand it and in line with RCKC’s comments at the meeting, is entirely bereft of any comprehension of &nbsp;Gramercy’s past behaviour, which we are not convinced RCKC has been fully made aware of or appreciated. The inclusion of clause 5.12(b) and the fact that this clause has been included and accepted by the Board prior to execution (and under the ‘new regime’) causes us serious concern. &nbsp;Note that for the avoidance of doubt, we understand RCKC to have been instructed on the construct of the actual clauses, as opposed to the performance of such clauses; &nbsp;(b)&nbsp;&nbsp; It ignores the Board’s lack of understanding, or in the alternative, highlights the lack of due diligence (which appears to have been intentional given comments by Howard and Joseph that they simply relied upon the previous regime’s negotiations and representations) when executing G3, something which is particularly troubling given G3’s importance and the fact that a member of the “old regime” continues to sit on the new Board. It is difficult for us to accept that such reliance has been given on an individual’s prior assessment of the inclusions of clauses in circumstances where the same individual was then suspended by the Board and remains (we assume) under investigation. This certainly was the case at the time we understand G3 was signed.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(c)&nbsp; The suggestion that professional obligations can/will be continually balanced against the need for funding, and “cured” by some form of protocol (which we note only applies to 5.12 (a)), keeps us in the same untenable position.&nbsp;&nbsp;10. Taken together, these issues solidify the risk of necessary and ongoing distraction from trial preparation. &nbsp;11. Of central concern is the sheer number of provisions we believe we cannot lawfully comply with, but which remain in an executed agreement. This puts us, and the company, at significant risk of being forced into an impossible choice in relation to various clauses of G3: &nbsp;Perform those obligations under G3 (for fear of reprisal that funding will be withdrawn due to a default of a clause of the agreement) and be in breach of our regulatory duties; or&nbsp;&nbsp;Decline to perform those obligations under G3 (because compliance would breach our regulatory duties), thereby jeopardising funding for our clients.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;12. All of the above makes the questions in our original email of 4 September 2025, 09:19, even more important, and our positions even more tenuous. &nbsp;13. There remains also our concerns and questions about the independence, structure and governance of the Board, as set out at paragraph 4(i) above and in the various letters written both by Defendants and PG employees, including Slaughter and May’s letter dated 13 August 2025 and letters dated 14 and 22 August 2025 attached to Céline Barnwell’s email of 5 September at 18:07. &nbsp;&nbsp;Please let us know of your earliest availability to discuss these issues.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Best regards,&nbsp;Michael Burke, Priyanka Gladson, Melissa Ferrari, Matt Newbold, Matt Hunt and Georgina Fallows&nbsp;(NOx case leads)​​​​Georgina&nbsp;FallowsLegal&nbsp;Director&nbsp;"}

