sonal information we collect and why, please see our&nbsp;Privacy Policy.From: Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Date: Tuesday, 21 October 2025 at 15:12To: Urquhart, Charles &lt;charles.urquhart@clydeco.com&gt;, Raminder Grewal &lt;rgrewal@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc: Riceman, Sarah &lt;Sarah.Riceman@clydeco.com&gt;, Lawrence, Jonathan &lt;Jonathan.Lawrence@clydeco.com&gt;, Fergal Cathie &lt;Fergal.Cathie@clydeco.com&gt;Subject: RE: Report [CC-UNITEDKINGDOM.131211.10761935.FID8873487]Thanks Charles. &nbsp;​​​​Helena&nbsp;YearwoodRisk&nbsp;&amp;&nbsp;Compliance&nbsp;DirectorFrom: Urquhart, Charles &lt;charles.urquhart@clydeco.com&gt; Sent: 21 October 2025 13:01To: Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Raminder Grewal &lt;rgrewal@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc: Riceman, Sarah &lt;Sarah.Riceman@clydeco.com&gt;; Lawrence, Jonathan &lt;Jonathan.Lawrence@clydeco.com&gt;; Fergal Cathie &lt;Fergal.Cathie@clydeco.com&gt;Subject: RE: Report [CC-UNITEDKINGDOM.131211.10761935.FID8873487]&nbsp;CAUTION: This email originated from outside of the organization. Do not click links or open attachments unless you recognize the sender and know the content is safe.&nbsp;Hi Helena,&nbsp;Many thanks for your email.&nbsp;All understood. I will revert with a revised draft shortly.&nbsp;Best wishes&nbsp;Charlie&nbsp;Charles&nbsp;UrquhartPartner&nbsp;|&nbsp;Clyde&nbsp;&amp;&nbsp;Co&nbsp;LLPDirect&nbsp;Dial:&nbsp;+44&nbsp;20&nbsp;7876&nbsp;4215&nbsp;|&nbsp;Mobile:&nbsp;+44&nbsp;7810&nbsp;607&nbsp;355The St Botolph Building | 138 Houndsditch | London EC3A 7AR | UKMain +44 207 876 5000 | Fax +44 333 3000 232&nbsp;| www.clydeco.com​My pronouns are: he / him​&nbsp;Follow&nbsp;us&nbsp;on&nbsp;twitter&nbsp;@clydecoemp​​​​&nbsp;From: Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt; Sent: 21 October 2025 12:41To: Urquhart, Charles &lt;charles.urquhart@clydeco.com&gt;; Raminder Grewal &lt;rgrewal@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc: Riceman, Sarah &lt;Sarah.Riceman@clydeco.com&gt;; Lawrence, Jonathan &lt;Jonathan.Lawrence@clydeco.com&gt;; Fergal Cathie &lt;Fergal.Cathie@clydeco.com&gt;Subject: RE: Report [CC-UNITEDKINGDOM.131211.10761935.FID8873487]&nbsp;Hi Charles,&nbsp;Many thanks for your email. Raminder and I have discussed this morning.&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;We are aligned on responding later in the week and that we shouldn’t rush back unless Pallas has a strong view on this. &nbsp;Regarding the approach, we agree that maintaining a neutral stance is preferable, while also making it clear that malicious disclosures are not appropriate. The response should be provided from Alicia as COLP.&nbsp;We would be grateful if you could mark up the draft response in line with your suggestions and circulate it to Pallas/the wider group.&nbsp;Kind regards,Helena &nbsp;&nbsp;​​​​Helena&nbsp;YearwoodRisk&nbsp;&amp;&nbsp;Compliance&nbsp;DirectorThis email and any attachments are confidential and may also be privileged. If you receive this message in error, please contact the sender immediately, destroy the email and any attachments and do not use, copy, store or disclose this email and any attachments for any purpose. Pogust Goodhead does not accept service of documents by electronic means without express prior agreement.&nbsp;Pogust Goodhead is the trading name of&nbsp;PGMBM Law Ltd, a&nbsp;limited liability company registered in England and Wales (no. 6284764) with its&nbsp;registered office at&nbsp;53-64 Chancery House, Chancery Lane, London WC2A 1QS&nbsp;and authorised and regulated by the Solicitors' Regulation Authority. We use the term ‘partner’ to refer to an employee or consultant of Pogust Goodhead with equivalent standing or qualifications. No&nbsp;reference to a partner should be taken to imply partnership for the purposes of the Partnership Act 1890&nbsp;or Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000. A list of partners is available on our&nbsp;website.&nbsp;For details about what personal information we collect and why, please see our&nbsp;Privacy Policy.From: Urquhart, Charles &lt;charles.urquhart@clydeco.com&gt; Sent: 20 October 2025 18:05To: Raminder Grewal &lt;rgrewal@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc: Riceman, Sarah &lt;Sarah.Riceman@clydeco.com&gt;; Lawrence, Jonathan &lt;Jonathan.Lawrence@clydeco.com&gt;; Fergal Cathie &lt;Fergal.Cathie@clydeco.com&gt;; Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: FW: Report [CC-UNITEDKINGDOM.131211.10761935.FID8873487]&nbsp;CAUTION: This email originated from outside of the organization. Do not click links or open attachments unless you recognize the sender and know the content is safe.&nbsp;Subject to legal advice privilege&nbsp;Hi Raminder,&nbsp;Many thanks again for your email. I have now had an opportunity to review the draft response (and have spoken to Jonathan Lawrence who has in turn spoken to Fergal) and from an employment perspective I had the following initial comments (I have reproduced an extract from PG’s whistleblowing policy below which informs some of these thoughts):-&nbsp;Process: We are rushing back to them with an answer to all their points very fast and, as a result, there could be an allegation that PG has not investigated the matter properly and, as such, that they argue that PG have committed a fundamental breach of contract entitling them to walk out of their employment immediately. I appreciate that this is a weak argument, however we do not want to give them any opportunity here – particularly given that our threat would be to injunct them to hold them to their notice periods and PG were reluctant, from a commercial position, to do that with Gallagher. Query therefore whether it would be better to send a holding response now with a promise to investigate and respond fully as soon as possible - say by the end of the week? This would demonstrate that a proper process is being followed but that we were prioritising it such that they could not argue any delay. The only caveat to this, is a comment from Fergal and Jonathan which is that the implication that the Board might need to investigate whether Alicia (a member of the Board) had mislead the Court rather than knowing already that she had not. The way to mitigate that could be by saying that the Board consider the allegations are baseless on the basis of their current knowledge, but in order to follow their proper process, will investigate them properly in accordance with the policy.Strategy: Do we want to rush back in any event? Subject to your instructions, I would have thought that we want to string this out as long as possible (subject to not delaying too long as per point 1 above) so that they don’t jump ship. As I understand it, the longer we get into the NOx litigation the more difficult it will be for them to have the time for them to leave/ join JLG/ and get involved in the CRO.Approach: Although I entirely agree that Burke and co are most likely to be raising allegations maliciously and/or not in good faith contrary to the policy, (i) making disclosures in good faith is no longer a requirement to raise a protected disclosure; and (ii) given the risk identified in point 1 above I wonder whether, strategically, we should be so pugnacious in this response? Given that we don’t want to give them even half a chance to walk out I wonder whether adopting a more neutral approach is better at this stage? E.g. “thank you once again for raising these points. As you know we take any concerns raised very seriously and encourage employees to raise matters with which they are not happy. To that end we have thoroughly investigated your concerns and reached the following findings….” And at the end: “we trust that this answers your concerns.” For this response I would steer away from cross referencing Burke’s own email asking to leave early, beyond, perhaps, a general reference to the policy and that malicious disclosures are not appropriate that we could weave in. I appreciate that there is a balance to be struck here between shutting them down and not walking into a trap whereby they just up sticks.Form of response: Your whistleblowing policy does not actually require that a formal response be provided – just that an investigation is done. The extent of the obligation is: “and formal meeting(s) may be arranged to clarify what action or remediation measures will be taken”. This is entirely standard for a whistleblowing policy. Therefore, technically, no response need be provided. I appreciate however that this is a case where we do want a comprehensive response.Person conducting the investigation: There is no strict obligation to have an independent person conduct the investigation but clearly it is fairer if this is done. I appreciate that there may be little appetite however to have someone independent come in and look at everything&nbsp; - unless this would work better from a tactical perspective as it would build appropriate and understandable delay.&nbsp;Subject to your thoughts on the above, we are happy to mark up the response (to the extent needed) and prepare an initial holding response.&nbsp;Grateful for your thoughts on the approach.&nbsp;Best wishes&nbsp;Charlie&nbsp;PS Your whistleblowing policy states:-&nbsp;The investigation will be carried out in the strictest of confidentiality, not informing the subject of the complaint unless or until it becomes necessary to do so.&nbsp;Where allegations of abuse of an employee or client have been made, suspension from duties subject to a disciplinary process may be required. &nbsp;Within 10 working days of a concern being raised, the person investigating your concern will write to you:&nbsp;Acknowledging receipt of your concern&nbsp;Outlining how the firm will deal with your concern and the names of any other people who will be involved in the investigation (for investigation of criminal conduct or allegations of illegality, it may be necessary to report these in confidentiality to the police, who may decide to investigate, with the possible result that the law may require non-notification of investigation).&nbsp;Investigations may be carried out by&nbsp;•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Independent Inquiry•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Internal Audit•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; External Auditors•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Police•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Solicitors Regulatory Authority / Information Commissioner’s Office/Financial Conduct Authority&nbsp;You may be required to attend for interview, if so, details of support services and how you may be accompanied to interview will be provided beforehand. The degree of complexity of the matter will determine the extent of contact between the whistleblower and the investigating party.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Charles&nbsp;UrquhartPartner&nbsp;|&nbsp;Clyde&nbsp;&amp;&nbsp;Co&nbsp;LLPDirect&nbsp;Dial:&nbsp;+44&nbsp;20&nbsp;7876&nbsp;4215&nbsp;|&nbsp;Mobile:&nbsp;+44&nbsp;7810&nbsp;607&nbsp;355The St Botolph Building | 138 Houndsditch | London EC3A 7AR | UKMain +44 207 876 5000 | Fax +44 333 3000 232&nbsp;| www.clydeco.com​My pronouns are: he / him​&nbsp;Follow&nbsp;us&nbsp;on&nbsp;twitter&nbsp;@clydecoemp​​​​&nbsp;From: Raminder Grewal &lt;rgrewal@pogustgoodhead.com&gt; Sent: 20 October 2025 10:45To: Urquhart, Charles &lt;charles.urquhart@clydeco.com&gt;; Riceman, Sarah &lt;sarah.riceman@clydeco.com&gt;; Lawrence, Jonathan &lt;jonathan.lawrence@clydeco.com&gt;; Fergal Cathie &lt;fergal.cathie@clydeco.com&gt;Cc: Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: FW: Report&nbsp;Good morning all, &nbsp;Pallas has kindly drafted a response to CL’s email from Friday - can you please review it and reply with any comments. &nbsp;Thank you R&nbsp;​​​​Raminder&nbsp;GrewalGlobal&nbsp;HR&nbsp;Director+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;718This email and any attachments are confidential and may also be privileged. If you receive this message in error, please contact the sender immediately, destroy the email and any attachments and do not use, copy, store or disclose this email and any attachments for any purpose. Pogust Goodhead does not accept service of documents by electronic means without express prior agreement.&nbsp;Pogust Goodhead is the trading name of&nbsp;PGMBM Law Ltd, a&nbsp;limited liability company registered in England and Wales (no. 6284764) with its&nbsp;registered office at&nbsp;53-64 Chancery House, Chancery Lane, London WC2A 1QS&nbsp;and authorised and regulated by the Solicitors' Regulation Authority. We use the term ‘partner’ to refer to an employee or consultant of Pogust Goodhead with equivalent standing or qualifications. No&nbsp;reference to a partner should be taken to imply partnership for the purposes of the Partnership Act 1890&nbsp;or Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000. A list of partners is available on our&nbsp;website.&nbsp;For details about what personal information we collect and why, please see our&nbsp;Privacy Policy.From: Raminder Grewal &lt;rgrewal@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Date: Monday, 20 October 2025 at 10:42To: Xenia Kalognomas &lt;xenia.kalognomas@pallasllp.com&gt;, Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc: Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;, Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;, Plate Litigation &lt;plate_litigation@pallasllp.com&gt;Subject: FW: ReportGood morning Xenia, &nbsp;Thank you. I’ll take this forward with &nbsp;Clydes. &nbsp;@Helena Yearwood looping you in re the W/blower element fyi. I’ll pick this up with you too directly. &nbsp;Thanks, R&nbsp;From: Xenia Kalognomas &lt;Xenia.Kalognomas@pallasllp.com&gt;Date: Monday, 20 October 2025 at 09:49To: Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;, Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;, Raminder Grewal &lt;rgrewal@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc: Plate Litigation &lt;plate_litigation@pallasllp.com&gt;Subject: RE: ReportCAUTION: This email originated from outside of the organization. Do not click links or open attachments unless you recognize the sender and know the content is safe.&nbsp;Morning Alicia, Anna and Raminder, &nbsp;We attach a draft response to the email below for your review. We also think that Clydes should review before it goes. &nbsp;Separately, if you have a whistleblower policy or any other policy for dealing with protected disclosures, you should see if it contains a required procedure for dealing with such disclosures, and ensue you follow it (for example, the response may need to come from a particular person). &nbsp;Please let us know if you have any comments or questions. &nbsp;Best regards, &nbsp;Xenia &nbsp;Xenia&nbsp;KalognomasAssociateT:&nbsp;+44&nbsp;20&nbsp;4574&nbsp;1065|M:&nbsp;77&nbsp;0121&nbsp;8358E:&nbsp;&nbsp;xenia.kalognomas@pallasllp.com110&nbsp;Bishopsgate,&nbsp;London,&nbsp;EC2N&nbsp;4AYpallasllp.com&nbsp;|&nbsp;LinkedInPallas Partners LLP is a limited liability partnership registered in England with registered number OC437491, and is authorised and regulated by the Solicitors Regulation Authority of England and Wales (“SRA”) with SRA number 826251. A list of names of the members is available for inspection at our registered address at&nbsp;110 Bishopsgate, London, EC2N 4AY.&nbsp;The word “partner” denotes a member of&nbsp;Pallas Partners LLP&nbsp;or an employee or consultant with equivalent standing and qualifications.&nbsp;Pallas Partners (US) LLP is a limited liability partnership established in Delaware and registered in New York, located at 75 Rockefeller Plaza, New York, NY, 10019.&nbsp;Attorneys practicing law with Pallas Partners (US) LLP are admitted to practice in New York and their professional conduct is regulated by the New York Rules of Professional Conduct.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;This email (and any attachments) is confidential and may also be legally privileged and is intended solely for the use of the individual or entity addressed above. If you are not the intended recipient, please immediately notify the sender, then delete it from your system. You should not copy it or use it for any purpose, nor disclose its contents to any other person. Please also note that emails sent or received may be monitored to ensure compliance with the law and with our policies.&nbsp;Further regulatory information can be found in the Legal Notices which appear on our website at&nbsp;https://www.pallasllp.com/legal.​​​​From: Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt; Sent: 17 October 2025 13:28To: Plate Litigation &lt;plate_litigation@pallasllp.com&gt;Cc: Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: Fw: Report&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Sent from Outlook for iOS​​​​Alicia&nbsp;AliniaChief&nbsp;Executive&nbsp;Officer+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;629This email and any attachments are confidential and may also be privileged. If you receive this message in error, please contact the sender immediately, destroy the email and any attachments and do not use, copy, store or disclose this email and any attachments for any purpose. Pogust Goodhead does not accept service of documents by electronic means without express prior agreement.&nbsp;Pogust Goodhead is the trading name of&nbsp;PGMBM Law Ltd, a&nbsp;limited liability company registered in England and Wales (no. 6284764) with its&nbsp;registered office at&nbsp;53-64 Chancery House, Chancery Lane, London WC2A 1QS&nbsp;and authorised and regulated by the Solicitors' Regulation Authority. We use the term ‘partner’ to refer to an employee or consultant of Pogust Goodhead with equivalent standing or qualifications. No&nbsp;reference to a partner should be taken to imply partnership for the purposes of the Partnership Act 1890&nbsp;or Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000. A list of partners is available on our&nbsp;website.&nbsp;For details about what personal information we collect and why, please see our&nbsp;Privacy Policy.From:&nbsp;Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;Friday, October 17, 2025 1:22 pmTo:&nbsp;Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Howard Morris &lt;hmorris@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Joseph Moreno &lt;jmoreno@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Priyanka Gladson &lt;pgladson@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Sarah Bingham &lt;sbingham@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;Report &nbsp;Dear Board,&nbsp;We write further to the emails exchanged between Michael Burke, Sarah Bingham and Anna Varga dated 15 and 16 October 2025 as they relate to the Litigation Management Agreement (&quot;LMA&quot;)&nbsp;and Agency Agreement (&quot;AA&quot;). This email is intended to be a protected disclosure.&nbsp;&nbsp;As can be seen from the First Witness Statement of Alicia Alinia dated 30 September at paragraph 35.1 - she has confirmed (and verified with a statement of truth): &quot;For the avoidance of doubt, Gramercy has no control over the day-to-day management or decisions of the Firm or the Partner, and nor does Gramercy have any role in the conduct, control or management of the NOx Group Litigations.&quot;&nbsp;Further, at 35.3, it is confirmed that &quot;Its board of directors is entirely independent of Gramercy, and nothing in its financial arrangements inhibits the Firm from actively, and independently, pursuing the Proceedings in the best interests of its clients.&quot;&nbsp;On account of the email sent by Anna Varga on 16 October at 8.57AM in which she explicitly instructs Sarah Bingham to instruct Stewarts (who are retained by Gramercy - see email from Joshua Katz dated 13.05.2025 confirming this) to provide input into documents central to the NOx Litigation - the statement at 35.1 is now categorically untrue. Senior management has instructed a member of staff to engage with a firm which is instructed by Gramercy to review both Pan NOx and PG specific documents. This position therefore must be corrected to the Court and to the Defendants (and to the PG staff) where the firm have represented that Gramercy does not have input into the conduct of the&nbsp;Litigations (in&nbsp;the case of to the Court - with a signed statement of truth).&nbsp;&nbsp;With regard to 35.3 - see the email from Julian Chamberlayne on 9 September 2025 at 11.30 in which he confirms that &quot;I have now heard from Gramercy that, based on our review, they are content with you proceeding with the revised agency agreements&quot;&nbsp;. In circumstances where Stewarts, instructed by Gramercy, are reviewing and providing approval in respect of key NOx documents and requiring PG to await execution prior to their approval, it cannot be true to state that the Firm can independently pursue the Proceedings (and is in fact doing so) cannot be true.&nbsp;We completely understand that a litigation funder&nbsp;will have a legitimate interest in reviewing certain documents, and may quite properly wish to have input where those documents either (a) give rise to a financial liability on the funder, or (b) relate to compliance with the funding terms.We also acknowledge PG’s position under the funding arrangements in particular, the covenant requiring PG to obtain the funder’s prior approval before engaging any new law firm or barrister to whom PG grants a contingent interest. This requirement exists because such engagements could create a financial exposure that Gramercy may ultimately need to meet.However, the Pan-NOx Agency Agreement&nbsp;does not&nbsp;fall within that category. It does not create any contingent interests, nor does it involve any cost contribution obligations from the Non-Lead Firms. Its purpose is purely to define the principal–agent relationship&nbsp;between Pogust Goodhead (as Lead Solicitor) and the Non-Lead Firms, governing the conduct, management, strategy decisions and coordination of the litigation. In short, it enables PG to act independently as Lead Solicitor within the proceedings.Similarly, the Litigation Management Agreement is not a funding or liability document. Rather, it is an agreement between the Client Committee and Pogust Goodhead, setting out the instructions, authority and governance framework&nbsp;under which PG acts for the claimants within each GLO. It defines procedural and operational responsibilities, not financial exposure, and is an essential component of PG’s authority to manage the litigation.The level of control that Gramercy has sought to exercise over these documents &nbsp;which govern solicitor-client relationships, not funder obligations&nbsp; goes far beyond the usual parameters of a lender–borrower relationship. It represents an overreach into the conduct and management of the litigation itself, rather than oversight of financial risk. That distinction is fundamental, and Gramercy’s continued involvement in these matters strays beyond what is permissible for a funder maintaining a proper arm’s-length relationship with the litigation.It therefore&nbsp;appears that our collective position is that PG is&nbsp;now openly operating in a world where the funder of the firm has direct management/control of aspects of the litigation - and that control is on-going, despite representations&nbsp;to the contrary made to the Court, the Defendants and to the staff. This creates significant issues for the staff working on these Litigations both in our duties to our clients and to the clients of the other firms in our firm's role as Lead Solicitors.&nbsp;&nbsp;Can you please advise on the following:&nbsp;why representations have been made to the Court, Defendants and staff that Gramercy do not have any role in the conduct, control or management of the NOx Group Litigations when plainly - they do and continue to do so;whether the Board intends to update the Court as to the true position; andwhether the Board intends to inform the clients and other claimant firms of Gramercy's input into documents that have a direct bearing on costs and client instructions.&nbsp;We are continuing to consider this on-going issue as more information comes to light. All of our rights are reserved.&nbsp;&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;Michael, Priyanka, Sarah&nbsp;​​​​Michael&nbsp;Burke(He/Him)Partner+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7960&nbsp;032&nbsp;34407960&nbsp;032&nbsp;344&nbsp;_____________________________________________________ If our account details change, we will notify these to you by letter, telephone or face-to-face and never by email. Clyde &amp; Co’s privacy notice can be found here Privacy notice: Clyde &amp; Co (clydeco.com) Please could we request that all correspondence with us is sent electronically where possible. For matters subject to the jurisdiction of England &amp; Wales and where you have agreed with the lawyer with the conduct of the case that email service is appropriate, please use this address service@clydeco.com for service, subject to the restrictions set out here . Please copy your email to the lawyer with conduct of the case and include our Clyde &amp; Co reference. This email is sent for and on behalf of Clyde &amp; Co LLP, a limited liability partnership registered in England and Wales under number OC326539 and with its registered office at The St Botolph Building, 138 Houndsditch, London, EC3A 7AR, United Kingdom (Tel: +44 20 7876 5000. Fax: +44 20 7876 5111). Clyde &amp; Co LLP is authorised and regulated by the Solicitors Regulation Authority under number 460690 and uses the word &quot;partner&quot; to refer to a member of the LLP, or an employee or consultant with equivalent standing and qualifications. A list of members is available at: www.clydeco.com. This email and any attachments are confidential and may also be privileged. If you have received this message in error, please notify the sender immediately, destroy this email and any attachments, and do not use, copy, store and/or disclose to any person this email and any attachments. "}

