Could you please provide us with an anticipated timeline for when the Court is expected to issue its order regarding PG’s position?
 Is it anticipated that the Court will order Leigh Day become the sole lead? Further, please can PG provide some commentary on the practical ramifications of them stepping down as lead solicitors? We note your comment that “The firm remains a fully active member
 of the Steering Committee and is in the process of agreeing mechanisms whereby it can continue work on the matter in substantive terms.”, which is helpful to understand. Could you please confirm what specific responsibilities PG handled in their capacity as
 lead, and the strategy for transferring these? Are there any unresolved tasks, specifically considering the proximity to trial?
Response:


The Firm has made an application seeking an order from the Court approving the Firm stepping down as a Lead Solicitor (in the Lead Cases only) at the Pre-Trial Review hearing on 6 October.
The application has been made on the basis that Leigh Day will remain as sole Lead Solicitor in the Lead Cases.&nbsp; We are not aware of any other Claimants’ solicitors firm applying to become a new co-Lead with Leigh
 Day, which would need to be made in order for any new firm to become a co-Lead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

The Lead Solicitor does not represent all the Claimants in the cases.&nbsp; The role of Lead Solicitor is to manage the Litigation on behalf of all Claimants, not just those who formally instruct this Firm.&nbsp; This means
 acting as a central contact point for the Court, including running Group claims registers, as well as instructing and working with a single counsel team on the common core issues for trial. The Lead Solicitor does not have authority to settle the cases on
 behalf of all of the Claimants.&nbsp; 
Consistent with the existing case management Court orders, arrangements are to be put in place with Leigh Day for the Firm to continue with its work as planned through the end of the PDD trial.
The Firm remains a Lead Solicitor in the Lead Cases until the step-down is approved by the Court.
&nbsp;


Who has authority to make a determination on settlement offers? Lead firm, Steering Committee, etc?
Whether the stepping down causes PG to lose or vary any decision-making power for their own clients and the wider cohort
Response



Nothing changes in terms of the existing requirements and procedures (under the Lead Case GLOs and agency agreements) for:
any settlement recommendations to be (i) recommended by the Steering Committee of firms in the Lead Cases and (ii) approved by solicitors in each
 case taking instructions from their respective client Claimants (including under existing claimants committee arrangements).&nbsp; This is because the Lead Solicitor’s role is to coordinate any such settlement discussions. The Lead Solicitor does not have authority
 to settle the cases on behalf of all the Claimants; and
management and co-ordination of the Claimants’ claims and conduct of all investigations, applications and proceedings, including preparation for the PDD Trial in the Lead Cases. This is because this responsibility
 remains with the Steering Committee (in which PG remains) with progress updates still being given to the wider Claimants Solicitors Group.&nbsp; Consistent with the existing case management Court orders, arrangements are to be put in place with Leigh Day for the
 Firm to continue with its work as planned through the end of the PDD trial. &nbsp;

