Subject: Hyundai-Kia Statute Bill


&nbsp;

Hi Matthew,


Jamie has passed on your request to produce a draft statute bill for Hyundai–Kia.


Please find attached a template final statute bill for Hyundai-Kia, based on the format drafted by Dr Mark Friston for the Medical Product Liability cases. This structure accords with the requirements for a compliant
 statute bill (i.e. a complete, self-contained bill of costs capable of triggering time for assessment). Tom Crow will be familiar with this template having populated several versions for the Transvaginal Mesh cases.


The bill will require three schedules:


Schedule 1 – Narrative of Work Done


This schedule must provide a clear and reasonably detailed narrative of the work undertaken (see
Friston on Costs, §51.20–51.25). It must cover:



Hyundai–Kia GLO-specific work; and
Work done within the Pan-NOx coordinated proceedings that benefited Hyundai–Kia claimants.

The narrative must be sufficiently detailed to constitute a “statute bill” under s.69 Solicitors Act 1974, i.e., capable of being understood without reference to the firm’s internal
 ledgers.
Someone in the Hyundai–Kia legal team can compile this information.


Schedule 2 – Basic Charges


This must show a breakdown of:



Hyundai–Kia common profit costs
Hyundai–Kia individual-client profit costs
Pan-NOx common profit costs, apportioned to Hyundai–Kia using the proportionate share of 4.29% (i.e. 78,779 claims relate to Hyundai-Kia out of the total 1,836,174 across all GLOs)
(please note the percentage has been updated,&nbsp;so you may need to amend the ‘Copy of 2025.11.05 Hyundai Kia Demand – In Hand Compensation Model’ spreadsheet).

The common costs are based on 100% of the costs incurred (subject to assessment) and 100% approved (subject to pro-rata allocation) in the cost budgets, plus any non-budgeted costs. It does not include costs incurred
 beyond the approved amount.


NOTE: Please ensure that any solicitor-own-client costs are identified separately if they are to be included in the statute bill. This is not currently included in the draft. The figure will be the balance per
 phase, where PG has gone “over budget.”

A statute bill may&nbsp;include both profit costs recoverable inter partes and non-recoverable solicitor-own-client elements, provided the bill is properly itemised (Friston, §51.13–51.15). However, given the 50% damages cap in Section I of the statute
 bill, it may be that no additional solicitor-own-client costs need to be included, as the cap is exhausted (Section J). It would make for a much smoother solicitor-own-client assessment (if there was one) to base the costs on the court-approved budgeted amounts.


&nbsp;


Section A – Profit Costs (figures to be used in statute bill)


The Hyundai–Kia common profit costs are derived from the attached breakdown ‘Schedule 2 Hyundai Kia &amp; Pan NOx Basic Charges’ of the court-approved cost budgets (then we net off the paid disbursements). The position
 is as follows:


Hyundai–Kia Common Costs



100% incurred costs (subject to assessment): £2,799,646.06 (inclusive of VAT and disbursements)
100% approved (budgeted) costs (pro-rated): £665,523.81(inclusive of VAT and disbursements)
Disbursements paid: £427,938.90 (inclusive of VAT)

(a) Therefore, the Hyundai–Kia common costs basic charges = £3,465,169.87 (inclusive of VAT)


&nbsp;


Hyundai–Kia Individual Costs


The individual client costs recorded to date on the Hyundai–Kia case code are:



Manual time recorded: £2,507,060 exclusive of VAT
Automated/system time recorded: £119,627,700 exclusive of VAT
Total: £122,134,760 exclusive of VAT

Total individual client costs divided across 40,763 Hyundai–Kia PG clients equate to:


(b) £2,996.22 exclusive of VAT&nbsp;per client


&nbsp;


Pan-NOx Common Costs



100% incurred costs (subject to assessment): £17,256,250.68 (inclusive of VAT and disbursements)
100% approved (budgeted) costs (pro-rated): £13,577,382.16 (inclusive of VAT and disbursements)
Pan-NOx disbursements paid: £7,460,175.97 (inclusive of VAT)

Therefore, the Pan-NOx common costs basic charges = £30,833,632.84 inclusive of VAT


Applying the Hyundai–Kia proportionate share of Pan-NOx claims (4.29%):


(c) £30,833,632.84 inclusive of VAT at 4.29% = £1,322,762.85 inclusive of VAT


Please note that the disbursements deducted have been calculated on the basis that VAT is chargeable at a rate of 20%. However, this may not be the case for all invoices; therefore,&nbsp;confirmation from Tom Crow will
 be required.


Figures (a), (b) and (c) can then be inserted into the statute bill at Section A (Basic Charges) - subject to the VAT status being confirmed.


&nbsp;


Schedule 3 – Disbursements


This schedule should show:



Hyundai–Kia common disbursements
Hyundai–Kia ATE premiums
Pan-NOx common disbursements, again applying the 4.29% apportionment

Attached are Schedule 3 Hyundai-Kia Disbursement Ledger at 271025 and Schedule 3 Pan-NOx Disbursement Ledger at 271015.


&nbsp;


Section C – Disbursements and ATE Premiums (figures to be used in statute bill)


1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
Hyundai–Kia common disbursements paid = £427,938.90 inclusive of VAT

2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
Hyundai–Kia ATE premiums paid = £234,840.00 exclusive of IPT

3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
Pan-NOx common disbursements paid = £7,460,175.97 inclusive of VAT but applying the 4.29% apportionment =£320,041.55 inclusive of VAT


Figures (a), (b) and (c) can then be inserted into the statute bill at Section C (Disbursements and ATE Premiums). Tom Crow will need to assist regarding what disbursements attract VAT and, therefore, what the VAT amount is, as well as the IPT amount on the
 ATE premiums.

We also need Tom Crow to confirm whether any disbursements or ATE premiums remain unpaid or are expected but not yet invoiced, as statute bills must disclose both paid and unpaid disbursements (Friston, §51.43).


&nbsp;


CFA and Scope


Attached is the Hyundai–Kia CFA, which is referenced in sections B and I of the statute bill. Please check with Dougie to determine whether all Hyundai–Kia claims are covered by this CFA, or if there are fleet/business
 clients engaged via different retainers. A statute bill must cover all work under the retainer, so clarity on the scope is essential.



Next Steps


This should give Tom Crow what he needs&nbsp;to begin populating the statute bill.

As statute bills and deductions are not an area in which Jamie has experience, I recommend looping in Jaspreet to work with the finance team going forward.


Please let me know if you need anything further.


Best,
Sarah


