Definition of Common Work. Per paragraph 5, PG will supervise its employees (including you during your notice period) in respect of
all&nbsp;work, whether delegated by LD or otherwise. This ensures continuity and means that you can continue to work on non-Common Work (e.g. PG only work) and anything else.
Pan NOx Agency Agreement. References to the Pan NOx Agency Agreement have been removed and the one definition used from it is now set out without reference to the Pan NOx AA. This had been the intention
 from inception but Martyn shared a working draft.
Step-Down Date. This has been
amended to indicate that the court order will approve PG’s application, not the resignation itself.
Clarification re. supervision.
As to your concerns about “dual supervision” by LD and PG and compatibility with ongoing professional and regulatory duties to PG clients, this has been resolved now by the latest amendments. These make clear that: (a) as long as you work for PG, you remain
 subject to PG’s supervision at all times regarding any work you do (delegated Common Work or otherwise); and (b) PG authorises you to do Common Work under the direction of and reporting to LD as LS.&nbsp; We have removed the word “solely” in both cases,
 to make clear that PG continues to supervise and be responsible for you.
&nbsp;
PG have taken specialist employment law advice. The agreement, and what you are being asked to do, is consistent with your employment agreements and your rights
 as employees.
&nbsp;
We have not accepted your proposal that PG personnel can also take direction from a third party authorised by LD. We can discuss this further, but PG needs
 to maintain knowledge and meaningful involvement/control over who can give directions to its personnel – and this is right now limited to LD in its capacity as Lead Solicitor, and a firm with which we have worked significantly on this matter. LD is content
 with this, and it should not raise any concerns regarding your workstreams. &nbsp;&nbsp;
&nbsp;
As concerns asking junior members of staff to send out correspondence, there is nothing inappropriate about this - and while ideally correspondence would be
